course: everyday
lesson: 22
title: Meeting a dog safely
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Before you touch any dog, even a friendly-looking one, ask the owner «May I pat your dog?» and wait for a yes; if there is no owner, do not touch it.
explain: Then let the dog come to you: stand a little sideways, keep your hand low and still, and let it sniff you, because sniffing is how a dog says hello.
explain: If it stays relaxed and leans in, stroke it gently on the chest or the side of the neck, not on the top of its head, and never hug it or put your face close to its face.
explain: If a strange dog runs at you, do not run or scream: «be a tree» — stand still, fold your arms in close, look down at your feet and stay quiet until it loses interest.
explain: If a dog knocks you over, «be a rock»: curl into a ball on the ground, tuck your knees in and cover your head and neck with your hands and arms.
explain: Running makes a dog want to chase, and staring into its eyes feels like a challenge, so a still, quiet, boring person is the safest person to be.
explain: A girl in Sydney ran up to a dog tied outside a shop and reached over its head; the frightened dog bit her arm, and she needed a doctor.
explain: A boy in Dublin ran away screaming from a loose dog; the dog chased him, jumped up and knocked him down, but he was not badly hurt when he finally stood still like a tree.
fact: Always ask the owner before you pat a dog, and wait for them to say yes.
fact: Never touch a dog that has no owner with it.
fact: Let a dog sniff you first; sniffing is how a dog says hello.
fact: Many dogs do not like being patted on the top of the head or being hugged.
fact: Never disturb a dog that is eating, sleeping, chewing a toy or looking after puppies.
fact: Running away from a dog can make it chase you.
fact: Staring straight into a strange dog's eyes can feel like a threat to the dog.
fact: To «be a tree», stand still, keep your arms folded in, look at your feet and stay quiet.
fact: To «be a rock», curl into a ball and cover your head and neck with your hands and arms.
fact: After a strange dog walks away, move away slowly and tell an adult.
fact: Any dog bite that breaks the skin needs washing with soap and water and should be shown to a doctor.
fact: Bites from dogs can get infected and often need a doctor.
exercise: What should you do before patting a dog you do not know? | Ask the owner and wait for a yes | Walk straight up and pat its head | Hold out food so it will like you | Call it loudly so it comes to you
exercise: How does a dog say hello to a new person? | By sniffing them | By licking their face | By jumping up on them | By looking into their eyes
exercise: Where do most dogs like to be stroked by someone new? | On the chest or the side of the neck | On the top of the head, where you can easily reach | On the tail | On the face and nose
exercise: Why should you not hug a dog you do not know well? | Many dogs feel trapped and scared when hugged | Dogs cannot feel a hug through their thick fur at all | Hugs make dogs sleepy | A hug will make the dog's fur fall out
exercise: A strange dog runs towards you barking. What should you do first? | Stand still like a tree and stay quiet | Turn and run away as fast as you can go | Scream so the owner hears you | Wave your arms at it to scare it off
exercise: When you «be a tree», where should you look? | Down at your feet | Straight into the dog's eyes | Up at the sky | At the dog's tail
exercise: Why does running away from a dog make things worse? | It can make the dog want to chase you | Dogs only bite people who stand still | Running makes you smell like food | Dogs cannot see people who run
exercise: A dog knocks you down. What do you do? | Curl into a ball and cover your head and neck | Lie flat on your back and kick it as hard as you can | Get up and run as fast as you can | Grab the dog's collar and hold on
exercise: Why should you keep your arms close to your body near a strange dog? | Waving arms can excite or scare the dog | Arms close make you look taller and stronger to it | Dogs only bite people's legs | Arms close keep you warm
exercise: There is no owner with a friendly-looking dog outside a shop. What should you do? | Leave it alone and not touch it | Pat it quickly while nobody is looking | Untie it and take it for a walk | Give it some of your sandwich
exercise: A dog is eating its dinner. Why should you leave it alone? | It may guard its food and bite | Dogs cannot eat and listen at once | It will forget how to eat | Food makes dogs sleepy and grumpy forever
exercise: Why is staring at a strange dog a bad idea? | The dog may take it as a challenge | It makes the dog fall asleep | The dog will think you have food | Dogs are scared of the colour of eyes
exercise: How should you offer your hand for a dog to sniff? | Low and still, and let the dog come to you | Fast, right in front of its nose so it can smell you | High above its head | Waving it from side to side
exercise: A dog nips you and breaks the skin. What should happen? | Wash it with soap and water and tell an adult so a doctor can see it | Hide it so the dog does not get into trouble | Put a sticker on it and forget about it | Wait a week to see if it hurts
test: Sam in Denver sees a puppy on a lead outside a café. Its owner is with it. What is the right first step? | Ask the owner if he may pat it | Kneel down and hug the puppy | Pick the puppy up to cuddle it | Pat it before the owner notices
test: A big dog with no owner trots towards Mia in a park in Dublin. Which plan is safest? | Stand still, arms in, look at her feet and wait | Run to the swings as fast as she can | Shout and throw her shoe at the dog | Stare hard at the dog to show it who is the boss here
test: Which of these is the safest way to stroke a friendly dog after the owner says yes? | Gently on its chest after it has sniffed you | Quickly on top of its head from above | With your face pressed close to its face | By pulling its ears to make it play
test: Why is «be a tree» a clever trick? | A still, quiet person is boring to a dog, so it loses interest | Dogs think trees are their friends, so they will not bite a tree | Standing still makes you invisible | Trees scare dogs away
test: Leo is knocked over by a jumping dog in Auckland. What protects him best? | Curling into a ball and covering his head and neck | Rolling around and shouting | Pushing the dog's face away hard with both his hands | Lying on his back with his arms out
test: Which is true? | A dog that is asleep or eating is best left alone | Every dog loves to be woken up for a pat | Dogs never mind being hugged by strangers | It is fine to pat a dog without asking if it looks friendly
test: The strange dog sniffs Ava and walks away. What should she do next? | Move away slowly and tell an adult | Run off quickly before it comes back | Follow the dog to see where it lives | Call the dog back to pat it
test: A boy in Manchester holds his hand out high and waves it at a dog he does not know. Why is this not a good idea? | Fast, waving hands can scare or excite a dog | Dogs cannot smell hands held high | Dogs like it, so it will follow him home and be his pet | Dogs only like hands held in pockets
test: What should happen after a dog bite that breaks the skin? | Wash it with soap and water and see a doctor | Nothing, dog bites always heal by themselves | Cover it and tell nobody about it | Rub it with butter to soothe it
test: cute dog tied outside the shop, can i pat it | No, not without the owner saying yes | Yes, if it wags | Yes, quickly before it moves | Yes, on the top of its head
test: big dog running at me what do i do | Be a tree, still, arms in, look down | Run away as fast as you can, dogs are slow | Stare it in the eyes | Shout and wave your arms
test: knocked over by a dog | Be a rock, curl up, cover your head and neck | Kick at the dog | Roll around and scream | Lie flat with arms out
test: dog's eating, can i give him a cuddle | No, leave a dog alone while it eats | Yes, he will like it, dogs love cuddles | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, take the bowl first
test: dog bit my hand a bit, skin is broken, it's fine tho | Wash it and tell an adult, you may need a doctor | Put a plaster on it and forget all about it, it's fine | Lick it clean | Wait and see if it swells
test: big dog running at me, no owner in sight!! | Stand still like a tree, arms in, look away | Run as fast as you can to get away from it | Scream and wave your arms | Stare at it and shout
test: can i pat ur dog?? (owner says no) | Leave the dog alone, the owner knows best | Pat it quickly anyway | Ask the dog instead | Pat it when the owner looks away
test: my aunty said a girl hugged a dog at the park and it bit her face and she needed loads of stitches | Real lesson: many dogs hate hugs, so ask the owner and never hug a dog you do not know | Real lesson: all park dogs bite on sight | It proves only girls get bitten by dogs | It means dogs are fine to hug if they are small
test: kid in my class reckons if a dog chases u, run as fast as u can, dogs can't catch kids | Wrong; running can make a dog chase you, so stand still like a tree | He is right, children are faster than dogs over a short distance | He is right, if you scream while running | He is right, dogs give up after a few steps
test: Ben's scared of dogs and screamed at a puppy lol, everyone's copying him at break | Do not copy or spread it; being scared of dogs is real and teasing makes it worse | Copy him, because it is funny and harmless | Spread it, so he gets over his fear | Spread it, and say he cried as well
exam: It being given that a child wishes to pat a dog accompanied by its owner, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To ask the owner first and to wait for permission | To approach the dog from behind without any warning | To pat the dog firmly upon the top of its head at once | To hug the dog so as to show friendliness
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the posture termed «be a tree»? | Standing still, the arms folded in, the eyes directed at the feet, and remaining quiet | Running in a straight line towards the nearest tree | Climbing a tree until the dog departs | Waving the arms above the head like branches
exam: Identify the circumstance in which a dog ought not to be disturbed. | Whilst it is eating, sleeping, chewing a toy or tending its puppies | Whilst it is walking calmly beside its owner | Whilst it is wagging its tail loosely | Whilst it is sitting upon command
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance where a child has been knocked to the ground by a dog. | To curl into a ball and cover the head and neck with the hands and arms | To lie flat upon the back and kick | To shout loudly and strike the dog | To grasp the dog by the collar
